:: Chapter 1: Nightmare in the Making [loop1] {"position":"300,250","size":"100,100"}
– //Beast... Liar... I know what you are,// – She whispers, as her hands light up the candlewick, barely shaking.

<img src="https://64.media.tumblr.com/a65ae38175812a0e92f69782d6786ed6/527e9990a7192707-64/s2048x3072/137d754f4ff56d8e580068787bfa025a9815590a.pnj" width="666" height="auto">

(align:"=><=")+(box:"X=")[[Light up the candle]]



:: Chapter 2: Scope of the Issue {"position":"300,600","size":"100,100"}
<p>She jolts awake in the darkness of her room, hopelessly gasping for air as she feels her neck tensing and spasming under the ghost of a bell-ringer rope.</p>

<p><em>– Oh, Bondye and all your Iwa,</em> – she whispers, shaking off running sweat, – <em>What did that demon-</em></p>

<p>She catches herself, realizing she was still in her night clothes. Hastily, she finds her box of matches and lights up a candle — by cruel luck, the spirit candle being the only one her room has got for a midnight light source.<br />
As the thin line of smoke rose up from the burning wick, Willow's eyes immediately cross with a wandering ghost that occupied her chambers. The spirit now seemed to haste to make its leave.</p>

<p>– <em>Excuse me,</em> – she called it over, her voice only gaining a more serious edge as the ghost sped up in its fleeting, –<em> <u>Sir</u>. I <u>only</u> have a question.</em></p>

<p><em>– ...mmm, yes? – </em>it turned it's masked head hesitantly towards the True Sight bearer, hand ready on the doorhandle.</p>

<p>– <em>How did I... end up in this bed again? </em></p>

<p>Her question lacked that determined certainty she wished it would've had. The ghost, in turn, only cocked it's head in slight disbelief.</p>

<p>– <em>You just arrived yesterday, and already got yourself <u>that</u> wasted? Oh dear...</em></p>

<p><em>– ...what do you mean, yesterday? - </em>Willow suddenly arose, as if deeply troubled by it's words, – <em>The masquerade already...</em> <em>Is it not Sunday already?</em></p>

<p>– <em>Well, this makes it a question<strong><u>s</u></strong>, not <strong><u>a</u></strong> question, –</em> the ghost grumbled an excuse, making its way into the corridor, –<em> I'll see myself out.</em></p>

<p>Her weary eyes glide back to the candlelight, hesitating as to how to interpret the answer. Maybe it was still Saturday after all, and all of this was an aftermath of the Masquerade Ball? Maybe Grinmaw didn't intend to break her in its possession, as her vision seemed to lead on, and she just slept it off in the end? Oh, maybe, but... she still feels rather worried.</p>

<p>She picks up her twin doll from the desk ("for protection") and shuffles out of her chamber, past the painting room, to the grandfather clock standing between the exquisite aquariums. The leech taunts her restlessly, not unlike the demon that drives her dear friends insane with its twisted tongues and heavy whispers. But it's the candlelit dial that takes her aback as she reads the arrows: it hasn't even struck one yet. Evidently, it is dead of the night, which will presumably follow into a Saturday morning. This... this isn't right. She doesn't realize the depth of the furrow on her face before it digs with dull ache straight into her forehead.</p>

<p>A fleeting thought passes her, a courtesy of the benefit of a doubt. Would the devil fish happen to remember their encounter?</p>

<p>Well... Maybe it would. Maybe it wouldn't, and all of this wondering is just a waste of time. By the time her hand slid behind the painting to open the secret door and unseal the chamber, she still hadn't made up her mind about the situation.</p>

<p>But what else does she have left to do now, really?</p>

<p>She takes a step into the unlit room, silent, this time. The body of a deep-sea demon flickered in and out of existence as the candlelight shone through the darkness of the aquarium's waters. Its lure dangled steadily, lifelike, just about charming enough not to want to make out the features of the real fisher; behind the sharp row of needle-like teeth, its voice boomed with a boastful self-satisfaction. Seeing how it readily proclaimed itself over her like an owner praising their dog for rolling over, Grinmaw somehow appeared to her so much more disgusting and... frightful.</p>

<img src="https://64.media.tumblr.com/1ce98cb97ebd54b8a1edc7c9ea9930fe/cb91bffca67d28b8-23/s2048x3072/7e3a94070d83571c694d0d3de8daae6dfeea7360.pnj" width="666" height="auto">

<p>– <em><strong>Willow, child... So you come to see me again. How delightful.<br />
</strong></em></p>

<p>Her eyes furrowed in an instant. Cursed be her intuition, but this time, she was actually right.</p>

<p>– <em>What have you done, demon?</em> – she accuses him, candle unwavering in her iron grip. If it chooses to torment her again, she will certainly blow out the candle.</p>

<p>– <strong><em>Nothing YET, yet so much MORE than you ever could,</em></strong> – it purred, taunting; it waved its fins and displayed its sides as if puffing up with immense pride. – <em><strong>I have set up the stage for the greatest performances of all! Marquis surely would've been impressed if he had known what would play out right under his nose.</strong></em></p>

<p>– <em>What do you make of me?</em> – the voodoo queen barked, her shoulder tensing to match furious scowl, – <em>You have possessed me, but you don't own me. You don't own <u>them</u>.</em></p>

<p>– <strong><em>Except I DO,</em> </strong>– it beamed, it's teeth cracking open in a rugged, threatening smile, – <em><strong>and all YOU have left for yourself is to WATCH ME.</strong></em></p>

<p>– <em>You are a poison</em>, – she states coldly, her hand finding twin doll hanging off her belt, – <em>And I will put an end to you</em>.</p>

<p>– <strong><em>Oh, sweet, sweet Willow...</em></strong> – it spoke through a condescending chuckle, – <strong><em>I'm not the one you better fight. Have you forgotten the sight of your dear friends, dying? As much as I am to blame, I'm only playing a little part in this wild carousel macabre. Even if you COULD put an end to me, you won't even come close to resolving this nightmare.</em></strong></p>

<p>– <em>But it would surely help to rid this place of you.</em></p>

<p>– <em><strong>Then shall I torment Tequila with the sight of your body hanging off the bell?</strong></em> – it's roar lashed out in fury, – <em><strong>You naive child, claiming you'll bite the hand that brought the fraudulent likes off you up to my level! What makes you think you have even a CHANCE without me, girl? You'll DO and SUFFER as I tell you — you and her BOTH. For you are MINE, and I am HIS, that's what will happen. </strong></em></p>

<p>For mere moments, her hands tremble under the spiteful scream. The guppy's shadow refracts as the candlelight flickers, dancing across the wall behind in pair with the illuminating flame. She remembers she can still put up walls, albeit that solution would only serve temporarily.</p>

<p>–<em> I reject your influence, </em>– she states with her weak, mustered confidence, as she readies to blow her candle out. It's the only bit of agency Willow can hold onto as the demon stares her down with its ever-shining grin.</p>

<p>– <strong><em>You reject NOTHING</em></strong>, – it sings back at her with a sick triumph, amused that she even chose to defy, – <strong><em>You can fight this new purpose of yours all you'll like, but it will only make your suffering taste</em> so much<em> better.</em></strong></p>

<p><em>–Then what do you want of me?</em> - she finally snapped, her voice rising as her nerves begun to let out, - <em>You speak of purpose and tell me nothing of substance!</em></p>

<p><em>– <strong>For that </strong><strong>I've already shown you. Those whom you know will all lay dead before the day is due, –</strong></em> Grinmaw stated his part with calm detachment so sudden it made her second-guess it, – <em><strong>And if the tragedy is not to be averted, I will make sure you join them.</strong></em></p>

<p>– <em>You spoke, but did not clarify my question, –</em> she objected, only for the grim, toothed figure to immediately cut her off.</p>

<p>– <strong><em>I am no god nor a guiding spirit to chew that out for you, child, </em></strong>– the demon flashed a cruel, annoyed grin, – <strong><em>Perhaps, you ARE hopeless after all, and would not know how to stand up against true darkness of this place - so be it! You'll have to learn by doing. A humbling lesson for hot-headed likes of yours, yes? </em></strong></p>

<p>– <em>You're even more obnoxious than you're poison, –</em> she muttered under her breath, lowering the candle with quiet resignation as she readied to snuff its light out,<em> –</em> <em>...I'm afraid I <u>still</u> do not understand what's happening, and you refuse to be of much help.</em></p>

<p>– <strong><em>Lost in your search for answers, Willow? Then you'll step forth and witness so. I recall y</em><em>our dear singer friend shall perform on her favorite stage </em></strong>– it's last words echoed as its shape faded back into the darkness with the smoke<strong><em>— do NOT miss the show.</em></strong></p><hr>

<p>Willow's second awakening caught up with her much later as the clock was approaching eleven. It seemed the exhaustion from two days wrangling of a demon just about begun to set in, and it was a worrying promise of a burden if she wouldn't be able to rid herself of it's toxic presence soon enough. When staff courtsely brought her breakfast in bed, Willow finally felt she needed to pull herself together faster. She needed to warn Tequila of the evil presence that plagued her thoughts, but she wasn't quite sure what seeds Juju Guppy had already planted in her. As much as she hated it, her only reasonable option was, indeed, to wait and see.</p>

<p>She downed her wine-red gown and tied her corset up in a timely manner. With a swift motion, she put on the top hat, and, albeit hesitantly, adjusted her skull-like mask. It was, indeed, a masquerade still, according to the staff's brief reminder to put on the costumes. As she adjusted the last little details, the announcement informed her of the 7 PM show at the theatre.  <br />
"When was Tequila's performance planned to be held?", she wondered. Even though she remembered Tequila mentioning she was in a hurry for something, there was no announcement for her performance. On the other hand, though, she heard rumors before that Tequila's stage was, in fact, a Theatre Brutale's dedicated orchestra pit. Maybe a dedicated announcement wasn't needed at all?</p>

<p>In the corner of the mirror, just at the edge of her sight, she spotted a bored passing ghost snatching up her twin doll.</p>

<p>– <em>Hey!</em> – she protested, turning fully to face the intruder, – <em>What do you think you're doing?</em></p>

<p><em>– Pf-f, – </em>the ghost protested, throwing the fetish away with theatrical disgust, – <em>Like I even cared for your creepy stuff. This is the most disappointing lady quarter I've seen in years!</em></p>

<p>– <em>Keep your filthy hands to yourself, troubled pervert, - </em>Willow darted in it's back with a hint of annoyance as the unwanted visitor likewise made sure to slam the door on his way out. Her features softened fast as she picked up the sacred item, whispering it an apology as she brushed it off the dust.<br />
Oh, ghosts. She almost forgot what a nuisance they tend to be in this wretched mansion. </p>

<p>The titular Flame in the Dark made her way out of the bedroom and into the corridors, hastily thinking over what she should say. Tequila, emerging out of hers with the tail of the song still carrying on her lips, somehow managed to catch her by surprise for the second time.</p>

<img src="https://64.media.tumblr.com/80be62a3a995ba8e65339393bb1a933f/cb91bffca67d28b8-4e/s2048x3072/2c6bd2dc22af3fc05d05cb1378dc56099ab67277.pnj" width="666" height="auto">


<p>– <em>Tequila! – </em>she proclaimed, less of a welcome and more of a desperate call for her attention, – <em>Are... Are you in a hurry?</em></p>

<p><em>– Oh, hello, Willow, dear! – </em>The Southern Siren put up a hasty, tense smile, – <em>I'm afraid I have to dash. He'll start without me otherwise...</em></p>

<p><em>– Ah. Performance, I reckon? - </em>She tilted her hand with a hint of an awkward smile, – <em>Would you mind me for the audience?</em></p>

<p><em>– Why, you'd better catch the main event at the theatre. I need to practice, anyhow: things just fall out of my hands today... – </em>a weary expression that slipped onto her face suddenly turned alert, – <em>You don't have a comb that I could borrow, do you?</em></p>

<p><em>– I believe I do, – </em>she responded without thinking; the guilt over their past misunderstanding still weathered on her, – <em>Would you like to follow me?</em></p>

<p><em>– I'm a bit short on time... – </em>Tequila hesitated, but made her way over to her door without further convincing as the voodoo queen quickly went inside. As the woman of macabre tastes was looking through the drawers, they passed a few words for the sake of a small talk.</p>

<p><em>–Darlin', has anything been troublin' you lately? – </em>she inquired, peeking slightly into the room only to find her neighbor making her way back out. –<em> I can hear you at night. Talking to yourself, all cooped up in your room...</em></p>

<p><em>– </em><em>Well, –</em> Willow's voice caught up, more hoarse than she hoped,<em> – you know how it goes around here... There are ghosts in these walls, and they crawl into your head through your ear.</em></p>

<p>Accepting the boar bristle brush of dark lacquered wood out of Willow's hands, the singer couldn't help but shake her head wearily.</p>

<p>– <em>It's creepy, is what it is... Creepy!<br />
<br />
</em>In her weathered eyes, the reply read out: "I don't deny".</p>

<p>She watched Tequila made her way over to the concert hall, unsure if it's correct for her to follow after being told not to show up. The situation suddenly presented her with a certain moral conflict. Normally, she'd go to face off the demon held within the secret chamber; now, the visage of it's daunting teeth and commanding voice dreaded her enough to reconsider. It instructed her too see the show, yes, it's just... She hated the prospect of coming off creepy and wretched, acting not unlike the ghost she chastised. She locked her room. Took a little walk to the flight of stairs, eyeing the lit-up fireplace. Stopped by the double doors, listening in on ghost's excited whispers. Cheering for the grand show. Looking forward to hear the performance of their most beloved singer.</p>

<p>It might not be a <em>correct </em>thing to do, but <em>still</em>... She felt inclined to follow Juju Guppy's advice — much out of genuine curiosity rather than fear, as she had to admit.</p>

<p>Willow pushed the door open, giving a nod to the three onlookers that kept guard of the Southern Siren's sacred passage. It stings her to stand out in her intentions, so she lingers in a foyer with them for a few patient minutes, respectfully taking in the atmosphere of the music's great sanctuary and exchanging a few words. </p>

<p>– <em>Are you Belle's fan? – </em>one of the ghosts opens a conversation.</p>

<p><em>– Can certainly recognize my friend's talent, - </em>she stepped around the question strategically.</p>

<p><em>– Oh, what honor! –</em> a second onlooker chimed in,<em> –  So you two happen to be on more personal terms?</em></p>

<p><em>– Have you heard of the song the diva's performing? – </em>another cut off with their question, – <em>Rumor has it,</em><em> it promises to be rather scandalous!</em></p>

<p><em>– Scandalous? – </em>Willow raised a brow.</p>

<p>– <em>Freshly written as of tonight! Speaking of... </em></p>

<p>Muffled voices could be heard behind closed walls of the concert hall : a familiar ghostly exclamations, a male whom the Flame in the Dark had no recollection of, and a female one - unmistakably, Tequila herself. A particular tension echoes through their conversation as two strain to remain civil. Before she realized it, Willow excused herself and rushed towards the concert hall. </p>

<p>– <em>Please, don't disrupt the show! – </em>the last ghostly plea reached her on her way out.</p>

<p>Right. She probably better not. As she stood by the door, hearing the first notes fill the hall, the voodoo queen contemplated what would even be the right thing to do. She's no ghost, so it's too late to walk in, that's for certain.</p>

<p>– <em>What! How did you get this? – </em>an exclamation carried from behind the door, a sharp edge ringing in a tensed-up voice, - <em>This is a private song! I never w-</em></p>

<p><em>– Miss Belle, please! We've already started! – </em>the pianist cut her off slyly. A moment of heavy silence, as his hands keep gliding across the keyboard, is met by what sounds like unwilling acceptance.</p>

<p>A bad hunch quickly turned itself into a palpable sense of dread that hammered down so relentlessly Willow could practically hear it. Surely, it would be okay if she just took a peek...?</p>

<p align="center">"<em>Once I was in your arms, crushing grip of steel... Once you had all my heart."</em></p>
<img src="https://64.media.tumblr.com/91dd6c2eaa098cbead4329c5426d7d3b/cb91bffca67d28b8-8f/s1280x1920/5532c127072c91970eecb69c8ed608af8fa3fe5b.jpg" width="666" height="auto">

<p>She leaned down to the keyhole, awkward and unfamiliar position that made her feel far more wrong about herself with the sheer discomfort it offered. Framed by the stained glass and pulled-up curtains, she could see the Southern Siren on stage; she held her head up high, not giving in to her former tension, as she filled the halls with a tragic and temperamental soprano. Willow barely considered herself an opera enthusiast, but even she could recognize the performer was a cut above most. It was a beautiful song — of course, all Tequila's songs are — yet it also felt so much more... personal, so raw in it's delivery it could be compared to the most honest cry. An expression of self so personal she second-guessed if she was permitted to bear witness to it at all.</p>

<p>A theatre announcement briefly cut through the rehearsal, but failed to halter the play. It seemed, if anything, Tequilas poured even more into the performance now, as she was letting go of last hints of qualm. Overtaken by her music like a force unlike any Iwa, she moved in spirit and body alike as her head swayed and hands rose in rhythmic motion. </p>

<p align="center"><em>“...Lord knows where that is now. Now I see through you as though you’re made of glass. And it’s too late...”</em></p>
<img src="https://64.media.tumblr.com/539bf6ffc3cab5eb18ddfa7f4f29bbd3/cb91bffca67d28b8-ef/s1280x1920/4cab53077fbd9685900619a3e7829e1acfd80012.jpg" width="666" height="auto">

<p>With a bated breath, Willow could not help but watch as the frail body of the singer produced the sounds so powerful they shook the very glass behind her; a song so piercing and personal she had no doubt it held the power to exorcise the very spirit of a man that led her down misfortunes that compelled to write it. Like a heartbroken, tender swan, she let herself crash against the hard rocks, shattering her voice just like the hopes Lucas left unfulfilled. She lamented what was never to be, wallowed in her purest of sorrows, and captivated her with it's haunting beauty so much so that the voodoo queen almost forgot to breathe.  </p>

<p align="center"><em>"Oh, what a waste of tiiiiiime—"

<p align="left">Her voice climbed in a maddening trance of a song until it hit the peak of what was humanly possible to draw out of it; so clear and absolute it became the very idea of a sound, the very unfiltered expression of what a human soul can be. Willow felt as if she never even <em>heard</em> music before this very moment - nothing possibly stood to compare with the private confession she stole from right under her keyhole. And it felt as if the moment could stretch on forever; her, caught entirely within that state, so powerful she could make the walls weep.</p>


<p align="left">Until they came crashing down on her.</p></em></p>
<img src="https://64.media.tumblr.com/473d9be0b67beca2a710ed4c329529cf/cb91bffca67d28b8-84/s1280x1920/fffe6729d98d294e28cdde6636f3b8c09340e320.jpg" width="666" height="auto">


<p>Neither she nor the woman on stage could completely process the moment when the song turned to agonizing scream as the glass shards <em>exploded </em>and fell upon her with a force of a dropped guillotine blade, kicking her off her feet and onto the cold, hard marble. In a flash, her body turned bloody patchwork of lacerations, pried open and made see-through down to bone. Every place where a splinter found flesh, it embedded into her like a painful kaleidoscope; every place where a heavy piece met something softer than marble, it sheared.<br />
<img src="https://64.media.tumblr.com/b6b879915f18bdb3817997444c1d1da7/cb91bffca67d28b8-01/s2048x3072/a57a0fe8351a7c969a9dcc31d29eca4695e78362.pnj" width="666" height="auto">
The shock of the situation was just about enough for Tequila to drag herself up her feet. Before Willow had a chance to think, she flung the concert hall door open, rushing towards her.</p>

<p>– <em>Tequila! –</em> her voice turned a desperate, terrified plea, as she held her under her arm, dragging away from the shard-littered floor,<em> – Please, hold on!</em></p>

<p><em>– Why, I'm flattered that my marvelous performance had an audience of two, – </em>the gasmasked pianist commented flatly, clacking down the fallboard. He headed for the door, muttering, – <em>I'll come back for you two when you're done here.</em></p>

<p>She laid down the singer with outmost care, but every movement made by her only left the shards digging in deeper. The lacerations were so many there was no way to even start picking the pieces out. To her friend's weak pleas, she pressed her shawl against the gushing wounds, praying to buy her more time; there wasn't even a chance for proper bandaging. Willow seen her fair share of gore, memories of the black pigs offered up to Petwo Iwa's still fresh in her head. But this much blood, pooling from under her friend oh-so-quickly, turned the visage truly nauseating. </p>

<p>It didn't take much to deduce Tequila won't be making it. </p>

<p><em>– Stay with me, – </em>she begged, her voice straining from the tears, as she felt the pulse flutter and wane, as her eyes lost their shine and liveness, as she felt her arm clasping hers for one final moment before her hands lost their strength completely,<em> – Stay— please—</em></p>

<p>But there was no more answers to follow after this silence.</p>

<p>Hot tears streamed down Willows face, dropping onto the sickly cold skin, mixing together with blood and sending splashes flying across the patterned tiling. Willow's chest cracked open with a sound she did not recognize as her own, as she held the body up to her chest with as much of an embrace as the sticking out shrapnel allowed her. She wasn't ready to lose another one. This sick twist of fate chocked her; for all her affinity with the spiritual and the dead, there was no such miracle that would've brought Tequila back to her.<br />
Did she even deserve it, if she couldn't keep her around living?</p>

<p>– <strong><em>She sung well, very well! — Just as I taught her,</em></strong><em> </em>- a painfully familiar voice whispered in her ear with a husky laugh, so intimately close she could believe it sat right at her shoulder, – <strong><em>And it is now time for your graceful exit, too, child. Come and join her.</em></strong><em> </em><br />
When she suddenly felt a cold kiss of a glass shard right against her neck's hollow, it jolted her. It escaped her <em>when</em> Grinmaw managed to hitch it's ride. </p>

<img src="https://64.media.tumblr.com/408238f2d19019e1e23b68b9a4064539/527e9990a7192707-d7/s1280x1920/4ee92071ab6a188a47b5f9111e66d07f0a0343fb.jpg" width="666" height="auto">

<p>– <em>No... – </em>a desperate whisper escaped her lips, quickly turning into a shriek, – <em>NO! Demon! CEASE!</em></p>

<p>For a mere moment longer, her arms struggled against it's force with straining tremor; but alas, as abruptly as she got to show her resistance, [[everything came to a finale.|Chapter 3: Strike Back and Reprise]]</p>


:: Chapter 3: Strike Back and Reprise {"position":"300,800","size":"100,100"}
<p>She jolts awake in cold sweat, fingers pressing against the formerly pierced side of her neck with sheer survival instinct; her throat twitches, as if it's still suffocated by the fresh, spilling blood and a new grotesque wound at her windpipe. All she feels in her ungloved hands now is her racing heartbeat ticking away down the unharmed artery. It takes a moment before she manages to calm her body down, no longer pushing air through at a fight-or-flight rhythm. Another for a single tear of grievance to curl up at the side of her eye. One last beat, as her grip on the blanket turned steel, for her endured distress to turn into sheer smoldering rage.</p>

<p>How in Heaven and Earth and Bondye's grace did that demon even get to her?</p>

<p>She got up with a practiced, mechanical motion, firing up a candle with a steady, albeit tensed-up, hand. She paid onlooker ghost no welcome as it looked through her closet. Didn't bother to read the clock on her way to the painting room — just like the last time, both of the arms pointed upwards. As she made it inside of the secret aquarium room and the sharp teeth grinned back at the candle from it's pitch darkness, her disturbed heart forgot to rise and hammer with fear before her torturer.</p>

<img src="https://64.media.tumblr.com/1ce98cb97ebd54b8a1edc7c9ea9930fe/cb91bffca67d28b8-23/s2048x3072/7e3a94070d83571c694d0d3de8daae6dfeea7360.pnj" width="666" height="auto">

<p>– <strong><em>And she crawls right back to me, </em></strong>– Juju Guppy teased her, impatient and hungry for spectacle, – <em><strong>How did you like my—</strong></em></p>

<p>– <em><u>Why</u>? – </em>Willow's voice escaped her; not with intended fury, but hoarse, rash and vulnerable, as if it had a mind of it's own. Grinmaw watched it struggle like a fisherman reeling up a prize catch.<br />
– <em>Why would you force me to take my life? Why would you compel <u>her</u>? How did you even— You simply couldn't have...!</em></p>

<p>It watched her thrashing with a familiar sadistic pleasure, its held-back chuckle escalating into a nerve-wracking booming laugh as the words died at her throat. It leaned in closer, speaking to her with almost condescending pity.</p>

<p>– <strong><em>You're not THAT hard to crack, child, you know that?<br />
<br />
</em></strong><em>– Pardon?</em></p>

<p>As she took a step back, it narrowed its eyes into mere prying slits.</p>

<p>– <em><strong>You and her both.</strong></em><em><strong> Living with a weight on your hearts </strong></em><em><strong>that leaves your souls bare and oh-so-exploitable. I </strong><strong>don't even HAVE TO work hard to turn your sufferings against you — with all the darkness living deep inside you, you were already mere steps away from succumbing to them.</strong></em></p>

<p>– <em>No! </em><em>But... –</em> her voice hitched in a pained protest as her hands reached for the twin doll in an oh-so-familiar gesture; something that otherwise would've felt comforting, yet now only served to prove the demon's point. That cursed, evil thing claimed to weaponise her very grief. It's only waiting to turn over every corner of her soul for its twisted desires, even if half of that soul already was far beyond her reach.</p>

<p>– <strong><em>But of course, I wouldn't kill you just for the spectacle,</em></strong> – it makes it's smile smaller, if not pretentiously modest, –<strong><em> It's my job, after all, even if your sweet, sweet pain justifies the admission fee tenfold. </em></strong></p>

<p><em>– Stop, </em>– Willow cuts sharply, gathering back her resolve, – <em>I'm sick of hearing your taunting. You said you wanted me to break the mold, yet you <u>still</u> forced my hand. What was the catch? Get to the damn point.</em></p>

<p>With an expression of deep amusement soured by unknown annoyance, it flicked its tail, once, twice, considering it's answer thoughtfully. For once, Grinmaw was no longer laughing — rather, staring the voodoo queen down as if weighing the price of a pawn.</p>

<p>– <em><strong>How much freedom do you think I possess here, reduced to a pretty pet fish?</strong></em></p>

<p><em>– So you claim yourself to be a victim? – </em>she raised a brow.</p>

<p>– <em><strong>Never, </strong></em>– a smile flicked on it's face, – <em><strong>But here's a thing, girl — be it a victim or a tormenter, we all follow a script here. Alas,</strong></em><strong> </strong>– the deep sea monster tapped the aquarium glass with it's soulless lure of a guppy, –<em><strong> I can't exactly make edits. So here's a riddle for you : who do you think writes the plot, and how do you take over the ending?</strong></em></p>

<p>She contemplated the question for a minute, hating every answer she dared to arrive at. If it really needed her to break the vicious cycle, it just couldn't be Grinmaw. Lucas would never do such a thing to them. Eleanor wouldn't even know how to. And whoever she'd point to, she wouldn't know how to even begin answering the second part.</p>

<p>– <em>I take it, you're not telling me, – </em>she suddenly concluded, finding herself out of plausible answers.</p>

<p><em>– <strong>Then look around and find out,</strong></em> – it teased her back, – <em><strong>You sure claimed a lot about your swamp blood, so it's right about time you start living up to it,</strong></em><em><strong> "voodoo queen".</strong></em></p>

<p><em>– Do not speak of my blood, – </em>she said bitterly, fingers closing in on the burning wick, – <em>And do not pretend you know what toll it takes on me to die by your filthy hand just because you'd rather gamble away my sanity.</em></p>

<p>– <em><strong>Barely a royalty, yet such a spoiled brat, </strong>– </em>it snickered, spreading it's fins as if claiming stakes on it's territory, – <em><strong>Do not pretend like I OWE you anything just because I THOUGHT I saw potential in you. How about you first go and prove yourself ANY useful?</strong></em></p>

<p><em>– ...I'm trying, –</em> she looked away, her voice emerging wounded. Seeing her fold, Grinmaw couldn't resist sweet temptation to drive the pain home further.</p>

<p><em>–<strong> So far, even your dead sister has done WAY more than YOU. Better start taking notes, child!</strong></em></p>

<p>She gasped with hurt pride, a disbelieved, offended look cast straight at the demon; it's polished teeth only reflected back her helpless fury. It crossed her mind that there was no use to keep going with it — at least, no use for <em>her</em>. There was nothing left to be said and too much offence taken. In one determined (if not desperate) motion, Willow snuffed out the candle, leaving the ghostly visage of devil fish wane in the water's dark.</p>

<p>Then, she found her hand, trembling, covering her twin doll's eyes. She couldn't stand up for herself, or for that intimate part of herself — for as much as she hated to admit, the Juju Guppy held it's power over her. </p>

<img src="https://64.media.tumblr.com/1555973492fef03a5e3b684a1a6e39b8/527e9990a7192707-40/s2048x3072/4ef1e9e92cb60722e2d28e1e6acb4ec1a4e822f1.pnj" width="666" height="auto">

<p>The silence felt deafening. </p>

<p>Willow made her way back through the halls, weary shadow falling over her features. Her restless thoughts turned inwards and picked at the bones of every failure and mistake she so far had to sustain. She was missing <em>something. </em>There was not enough time, not enough understanding, or not enough resources, maybe. She needed a leverage to get back control over herself. And beyond that, she needed a way to protect—</p>

<p>As she ventured back into her chamber, it took a moment too long to realize that she wasn't alone there. Tequila stood patiently by the door, night dress and hair out of order, as if raised in the midst of her sleep for some very urgent business. Her face gave away a second of relief before it remembered to tense up; as she spoke, her voice caught between caution and genuine worrying. </p>

<p>– <em>Willow?</em></p>

<p>– <em>Tequila.</em> <em>What... brings you here?</em></p>

<p><em>– I wish I would know where you just went out to, – </em>she proclaimed with barely held behind distress,<em> –</em><em> First you stumble around in the night, then you say all these horrible, horrible things to yourself... How can I believe you after all this that you're doin' alright?</em></p>

<p><em>– Oh, – </em>she blinks, completely caught off-guard by siren's observance. But of course she would worry! All her talk to Grinmaw about the inevitable death, to a normal person, would be no more that no more than madman's words cast into an empty room. Of course she'd fear the worst...<br />
Willow's words fumbled defensively before she could think them through.</p>

<p>– <em>I know how it looks, but I swear I wasn't planning to—</em></p>

<p><em>– Still, –</em> she shut down her explanation firmly, but not without a due care. As she made her stride towards the Flame in the Dark, she looked into her eyes very carefully before her gentle fingers brushed Willow's arm like a priest confronting a sinner hiding from their confession. </p>

<p><em>– Surely you can understand how I wouldn't want to leave you alone right now. Mind we sit down and talk?</em></p>

<p>Willow stumbled to find any words of reassuring excuse, a convincing argument just to forget about the whole misunderstanding. Reluctantly, she followed her neighbour's lead to sit down on her bedside; the singer's weight dipped the mattress beside her, both far enough not to be touching shoulders, but too close to feel her own walls would hold.<br />
The voodoo princess couldn't find in herself enough courage to look up at Tequila's expression. She didn't speak to outsiders too often, but from experience she happened to have had, most had grown worried or disappointed with her over more... spiritually involved matters, not to speak of the topics that naturally came attached to them. She still worried she stretched Southern Siren's patience thin.</p>

<p>– <em>You don't have to tell me everythin'</em><em>, – </em>she spoke patiently, almost as if reading her mind, – <em>But I'd rather not return to my room wonderin' if I should've fetched someone.</em></p>

<p><em>– Whom would you bring?</em></p>

<p><em>– Lafcadio, or, perhaps, one of the Rockridges, – </em>she confessed, – <em>Dependin' on if you'd need to be talked or dragged out of committin' to somethin'. </em></p>

<p>The voodoo queen pressed her lips together with certain grief she couldn't express to her. Perhaps, fetching someone really would've helped the first time around — but she doubts now, with it's voice sitting this deep, anyone could have changed the outcome. As her hands rested on the twin doll again, Tequila's eyes followed, recognizing the meaning behind it's symbol, but hesitating to call her out for it. </p>

<p>–<em> ...Has the matter of your sister been troublin' you again? – </em>her question broke the silence almost apologetically.</p>

<p><em>– ...not this time, – </em>her answer came out more hesitant than she hoped it would. Curses. She thought she'd forgotten. </p>

<p>– <em>You know it wasn't your fault. </em></p>

<p>– <em>It's not about her, – </em>her hands clenched, as she finally found strength to look back up at worried southern woman, – <em>Though the sentiment hasn't changed much from before. </em></p>

<p>Tequila's brows furrowed slightly as her lips strained with a sympathetic sadness. Her fingers gently covered Willow's, as if to silently say she was there to listen. Willow, in turn, decided it wasn't beyond her right now to accept the offered comforting. </p>

<p>– <em>God knows it's not easy to speak about such matters, is it? – </em>the seer let out a humorless, tired laugh, – <em>I've been certainly tormented by some recurring visions. </em></p>

<p><em>– Like nightmares?</em></p>

<p><em>– You could put it this way.</em></p>

<p>Tequila's face formed a tired smile, eyes somewhat clouded with sorrow as she brushed her neck subconsciously.</p>

<p><em>– Well, that certainly would make us both, then.</em></p>

<p>– <em>Haven't slept much either, I take?  – </em>the dark eyes searched her features worrisomely.</p>

<p><em>– Little enough to overhear all your talkin', –</em> she dismissed with a joyless, short laugh,<em> – But go on. What did you see in those... visions?</em></p>

<p>Willow dragged a knee up to lean on, stubbornly staring at the candle abandoned by the bedside table. She felt hesitant, if not <em>afraid</em>, to turn her gaze inwards. She swore she could still feel it — the weight of the body in her arms made heavy with shrapnel, skin enveloped in blood that drawn out at beyond alarming rates, hot at first, but quickly losing what still made it living as it darkened on the marbled floor. The sheer mental image was enough to scar her; but to speak is to process, and processing is, unfortunately, what she couldn't keep going without. </p>

<p>– <em>...you, –  </em>she admitted hoarsely, her voice straining as if she wished not to believe her very own words, – <em>With a stranger from the staff sitting at the piano. You, up there on the stage.</em></p>

<p>– <em>Is that so?  </em>– she confirmed, somewhat surprised, – <em>Doesn’t sound so much like a nightmare</em><em>.<br />
<br />
</em>The attempt at levity fell unanswered. Willow took a moment to continue, going over the events in her head.</p>

<p>– <em>He... something-of-Clubs, if I recall it correctly... He made you sing a very personal piece. Something taken without a notice, so private it badly offended you. Something about a man you used to love — but bitter, very different from the one looping down at the bar...</em></p>

<p>Tequila's expression quickly shifted from amused to concerned as she was let on on the details. She retracted and took in a breath before bringing herself to ask:</p>

<p>– <em>You don't happen to recall anything about that song, do you?</em></p>

<p>– <em>I didn't get to learn it's name. It didn't have a repeating chorus — or, maybe, you just didn't get that far. </em><em>It was so touching and raw in it's sorrow it could make the walls cry, – </em>her dark eyes found Tequila's shoulder for reassurance, as she forced painful words out of her chest, – <em>But you kept climbing higher and higher, far beyond what I thought a voice can humanly do, until the very stained glass behind you shattered to scattered shreds.</em></p>

<p>– <em>Willow...</em></p>

<p><em>–You stood right in the way— </em></p>

<p>She dropped her face in the fold of her arm, as she felt a weight gently settling down on her shoulder. The voodoo queen's voice trembled pathetically. </p>

<p>– <em>...you bled out in my arms on that glass-littered stage, and I... I couldn't do anything...</em></p>

<p>– <em>Willow, dear... –</em> Tequila spoke softly, but grounding, as she rhythmically stroked her back, <em>– Look up. I'm still here, with you. It was just a dream.</em></p>

<p>– ...<em>What if it isn't? – </em>her trembling voice shot up sharply, enough to make the singer freeze up. They looked at each other for a long, awkward moment, before Belle regained her composure.</p>

<p><em>– But </em>of course<em> it is. Redd is scheduled on the piano, as usual, and we're got "Tequila at Noon"  approved for our rehearsal. You have nothin' to worry about. You hear me, darlin'?</em></p>

<p>For a moment, she really wanted to buy into singer's unwavering confidence. But the truth, alas, just hurt too much.</p>

<p>– ...<em>and what about that song? – s</em>he inquired, no less worrisome.</p>

<p>– <em>Nothin' for you to concern yourself over, –  </em>Tequila assured her back somewhat coldly, folding her hands on her lap in a gesture of retreat.</p>

<p>– <em>You won't sing it, right? Please— promise me you won't sing it.<br />
</em></p>

<p>– <em>Of course not. </em><em>I'd never. It's far too personal, and... oh, darn it, –</em> she shook her head with light annoyance as she felt herself caught,<em> –  How </em>did<em> you find out about it, even? </em>I <em>forgot that it even </em>existed.</p>

<p>Willow straightened out, her eyes snapping to the singer almost in sheer shock as the pieces of Grinmaw's orchestrating finally fell back in place.</p>

<p>– <em>Did you...?</em></p>

<p><em>– Stayed up late to write it down right now, yes, –  </em>she darted eyes away, her lips tensing, – <em>Though, with all your talk, I'm startin' to regret that I did.</em></p>

<p>– <em>Destroy it, – </em>she pressed. Tequila didn't answer at once, weighing it against herself carefully.</p>

<p>– ...<em>I'll see in the mornin', –  </em>she finally settled decisively, standing up from the bed, – <em>But I think some good sleep would be very much needed right now. </em>Especially <em>for the worrisome likes of you. Take care, darlin', will you?<br />
</em></p>

<p>She exchanged a tired smile and goodnights with Willow before she found her way out of her bedroom. The voodoo queen listened carefully as she made her way through the corridors back to her own modest room. Heard the desk open. Then close. Then woman laying to sleep. All of a sudden, Willow felt as if she let out a breath she was holding since she found singer's sudden company awaiting back in her chamber.<br />
She made peace with the loneliness as she put herself back to bed, twin doll clutched close to her heart. Sleep did not come easy.</p><hr>

<p>It was the sudden scraping of the skeleton key in the lock and the rustling of paper that shook Willow awake. She couldn't tell if it was early morning, midday, or barely an hour past she got knocked out — her head was certainly splitting with a sleep-deprived (if not also demon-induced) headache. Startled, she sat up on the bed, trying to pry open her eyes.</p>

<p><em>– What a splendid material... This will be scandalous! – </em>a gleeful, muffled voice barely penetrated the door, and her heart sank.<br />
She've heard this voice before. </p>

<p>In a haze, she crossed her room, pulling down the doorhandle with a new sense of urgency — only to come face to face with a Seven Clubs. His posture shifted in surprise as he clutched a paperstack close to his chest.</p>

<p>– <em>Oh! Didn't expect to see you, miss, –</em> the masked man gave her a strained, polite greeting, <em>– Have I witnessed</em><em> Flame in the Dark on her titular sleepwalking? I can bring you back to your room.</em></p>

<p>– <em>You...– </em>she pointed at him accusatory, furrowing, – <em>You took this from her room.</em></p>

<p><em>– Ah, –</em> his voice turned malevolent pity, <em>– You must be suffering from your visions again, miss.</em></p>

<p><em>– Do you claim I am hearing things, too?</em></p>

<p>They stared at each other, standing off in a charged silence. Seven Club's chest seemed to puff up as he strung his words.</p>

<p>– <em>Perhaps you are. I understand that you must feel confused, but I assure you — we're just staff, doing our job.</em></p>

<p><em>– What is the song you fetched, then?</em></p>

<p><em>– "Tequila At Noon".</em></p>

<p><em>– You liar.</em></p>

<p><em>– Pardon, miss?</em></p>

<p>There was only a moment of delay between the furrow deepening on Willow's face and her hand moving to snatch away the papers. As her fingers creased one in her owl-like grip, she felt sharp pain up her wrist as the masked man quickly intercepted attempted reclamation. </p>

<p>– <em>Not so fast, witch, – </em>he hissed at the voodoo queen, twisting her hand out in a practiced gesture. Her breath hitched, as Seven Clubs suddenly overpowered her, showing her onto her knees. The man turned on his heels, rapidly putting distance between the two of them.</p>

<p>– <em>Hey! </em>– she called out, her heart still racing. Before she could come half the way through the corridor, the gas masked staff member disappeared behind the hallway door. It took her seconds to realize that pursuing him was, at best, optimistic; a few steps more for the inflicted pain to return, burning her like a stigma.<br />
That demon! That heathen...! But what else must she do...?</p>

<p>– <em>...was that you, Willow? – </em>a newly awakened, and, at that, reasonably startled voice finally called out to her. It dawned upon her that she was right under Tequila's door. But of course<em>— </em>She can show her<em>— </em>She will believe!</p>

<p>With the determined swing that set it open, the door didn't even have a chance to creak. The diva was half sitting on her bed, fiddling to turn on the bedside lamp. The voodoo queen's appearance, backlit by the dim halfway, finer lines ruffled, appeared almost ghostly. </p>

<p>– <em>There was a man in your room,</em> – she whispered hastily, eyes opened wildly, – <em>Just now. He—</em></p>

<p><em>– Willow. Stop, – </em>Tequila rose up a finger, her tired, barely awakened eyes clearly struggling to focus. She waved her to close the door and come closer, all of which Willow followed with no objections.</p>

<p>– <em>Look, –</em> she points at the dial of her alarm clock, not really meaning to shame her, but clearly sounding upset, – <em>It's five in the mornin'. I don't know what you were gettin' up to, but all of this is <u>really</u> troublin' me.</em></p>

<p>In a gentle glare of a lamp, Tequila's sleep-shrouded eyes finally managed to focus on a distressed form of her friend. She breathed heavily, startled, or, perhaps, chased. Her arms still tensed up from an unknown struggle. Fresh streaks of red, left by a steel grip of someone's fingers, bloomed on her right wrist. All of this combined painted pictures that left her heart sink.</p>

<p>– <em>Jesus, – </em>an involuntary whisper escaped Tequila's lips, – <em>D</em><em>id you just fight someone?</em></p>

<p>– <em>I.. I told you! – </em>she spat out with near paranoid worry, – <em>The man in the mask, and—</em></p>

<p><em>– Darlin', – </em> her hand carefully covered the bruise mark, <em>– </em><em> Please. Calm down. <u>Who</u> did this to you?<br />
– ...Seven Clubs, –</em> she whispered, <em>– Some new staff member.<br />
– How could he <u>dare</u> lay a finger on you? –</em> she spoke with quiet rage, more a statement than really a question.<br />
<em>– I saw him carrying music sheets out of your room. He got defensive when I questioned him. I...<br />
– You don't have to explain yourself. I don't need an excuse to rise hell over somebody treating a woman like this.</em><em><br />
</em></p>

<p>Willow almost recoiled with surprise when she heard the seriousness of intention in her friend's voice. This isn't what she wanted. She was supposed to protect her, not to lead her back to her death.</p>

<p>– <em>But... –</em> her voice caught, quickly racing in panic, –<em> You think he wouldn't get violent</em><em>? I don't want you to— </em></p>

<p><em>– Which is </em>why<em> I'll get Redd or Lucas involved, –</em> she stated coldly and calmly, as if the plan was crystal-clear to her,<em> – Can't imagine that fool abusin' his position for much longer after I have a word with them. Besides, you said he stole somethin' from my room, correct?  I can't let staff go around exposing my private matters, either.</em></p>

<p>Willow's mouth opened and closed helplessly as she failed to come up with any arguments that could challenge her determination. She watched Tequila rise from the bed and move to her writing desk, rustle inside of the shelf compartment for a few long seconds, and, finally, let out an irritated sigh.</p>

<p>– <em>I will be honest, –</em> she resigned, turning her head back to Willow, hands empty, <em>– I didn't want to trust your intuition, but I re-hid it between some old lyrics, just to be certain. And yet still,</em> somehow...<em> How did </em>he<em> even know?</em></p>

<p>Tense silence fell between them two, as both struggled to come up with a probable answer. A minute or two later, they realised that, perhaps, early morning after sleepless night was a bad time to solve sudden mysteries. They waved each other goodnight for the last time, with unspoken arrangement to check up on each other at noon. Against their best wishes, both were left with more questions than answers. </p><hr>

<p>– <em>I couldn't find him, – </em>she confessed with a bitterness of defeat, – <em>In fact, no one seen Lucas anywhere around the mansion. </em></p>

<p><em>– And what about Redd? – </em>Willow insisted. </p>

<p>– <em>Haven't ran into him </em>yet<em>, but I passed on a word to his brother. Clay will deal with the brute right after the closin' party.</em></p>

<p><em>– You rehearsal isn't waiting until then.</em></p>

<p><em>– I don't have it in me to argue with the former head of security about his approaches, – </em>she sighs, voice tensing, – <em>Besides, he already scheduled a drinkin' spree. I'm not in any position to distract him from that.</em></p>

<p>Willow's face furrowed under the mask worrisomely. Catching that, Tequila gently brushed her arm in a reassuring gesture — straying just below the bruised edge that poke above her glove edges.</p>

<p>– <em>I hope you understand I can't sit back and hide in my room. Not when my personal work is stolen. And </em>especially <em>not when you have been treated so poorly over gettin' it back.</em></p>

<p>Somehow, with the image of shattering glass still fresh in her memory, that didn't sound comforting. </p>

<p>– <em>...why does the last part matter so much for you? – </em>she protested weakly, – <em>I'd rather not be avenged.</em></p>

<p><em>– And I'd rather you didn't try to stand up for me all on your own, – </em>she confessed with a faint smile, – <em>But you're just too invested to stay out of this, aren't you?</em></p>

<p><em>– Forgive my stubbornness for not wanting to see you get hurt.</em></p>

<p><em>– Don't you think I wish the same for you, darlin'? –</em> her thumb insistently brushed against Willow's gloved hand, leaving dull ache for an aftertaste, –<em> Don't get me all wrong, but all your noble endeavor only left you with needless guilt and awful bruises. Don't you need rest, too?</em></p>

<p>– <em>I cannot rest tormented over what that demon did to you.</em></p>

<p>Tequila watched her for a long moment. Her brows creased, not with anger, but, rather, affection made uneasy by disapproval.</p>

<p>– <em>You really mean that, don't you?</em></p>

<p>– <em>Every word.</em></p>

<p>Tequila's gaze trailed off, her expression shifting into something beyond Willow's own understanding. Her voice followed unrushed, unexpectedly earnest.</p>

<p>–<em> Very well. I'll believe you on</em> that, <em>but I wish you'd trust in me, too. </em><em>That song is a very private matter to me. It would hurt me as much to know it's in someone's hands as it would you if </em>she – at that, Tequila pointed at the twin doll still fastened to the voodoo queen's hip, – <em>would've gone missing. I'll just</em><em> set the record straight, and I'll be out of there. Myself.</em> Alive.<em> Would you take up my promise on it?</em></p>

<p>For a few seconds, the only answer she could muster was silence. Then, finally, Willow let out a defeated breath.</p>

<p>– <em>Please, stay safe, – </em>she whispers, more a prayer than a plea.</p>

<p>– <em>That settles it, – </em>the singer offered her a smile, squeezing her hand one last time, – <em>You'll see me after the show, darlin'. Don't find yourself in any more trouble</em><em>.</em></p>

<p>Her steps echoed through the corridors, right until they disappeared behind the door she saw Seven Clubs escape through early this morning. No matter how much Tequila tried to reassure her, she couldn't knock off her own unease. There was no guarantee things wouldn't turn sour just because she swore not to sing — especially if that man is determined to see her dead <em>that</em> much, and does not stand above straight physical violence. She could bring herself to imagine hundred deaths that this woman might suffer; hundred deaths more that the devil would whisper in her ear for it with uncontained glee. She has to do <em>something</em>. But what...?</p>

<p>Her train of thoughts got interrupted by a ghost emerging from Tequila's own room with a neatly folded invitation. She recognised him as a pervert that ogled the singer on many occasions — one naughty soul she struggled to set straight however many times she tried. Noticing the voodoo queen's stare, he coughed in his fist, complaining:</p>

<p>– <em>Are you getting your kicks from judging others? Honestly, you and those three...</em></p>

<p>– <em>Where are you heading now? – </em>she acknowledged him with the same lack of enthusiasm. </p>

<p>– <em>To the concert hall. Obviously. </em></p>

<p><em>– There's not much to see there today, I'm afraid.</em></p>

<p>His eyebrow shot up a notch, as his voice challenged her in disbelief. </p>

<p><em>– Oh</em> please. <em>You've been chasing her around like a madman since early morning.</em><em> You don't tell me </em>you'd<em> skip the show just because she told you to, would you?</em></p>

<p>For a moment, her blank stare looked straight through the ghostly body, past the walls of Tequila's chamber and straight into the aquarium glass. Right. There <em>was </em>one more thing she could do, with all her limited power — and this bastard just gave her all the right ideas.</p>

<p><em>– I hate to agree with </em>you<em>, of all people, but... –</em> an involuntary smile touched her lips, suddenly finding herself all more capable, –<em> You know what? You're right. I'm not sitting it out. I </em>will <em>be there for her.</em></p>

<p><em>– Alright! –</em> he huffed out a pleasantly surprised laugh, –<em> Maybe you're not half as boring as I thought of you, witch. Well, then, shall we?</em></p>

<p>She watched the dead man parade forward with a swagger of someone who lost shame and regrets long ago. He begrudgingly reminded the voodoo queen of her seanses with Baron Samedi — except only the bad parts, with no promise of help or good resolution for the behaviour suffered. Or, maybe, the demon fish was right about her needing some humbling; sometimes, you have to make work with exactly what was given to you.</p>

<p>– <em>Do ghosts go by any names around here? – </em>she attempted to break the silence between them. The masked figure eagerly waves off the question before it thinks through it's answer.</p>

<p>– <em>Nah, not really. I mean, plenty of us are some big,  loud shticks that never shut up about their origin — like Alexander Minski or Mac Rockefella — but anyone would tell you that your history matters so little once you're already dead. I, personally, find a lack of name quite liberating!</em></p>

<p><em>– And you definitely lavish in the liberties... –</em> she scoffs, rolling her eyes.</p>

<p>– <em>Sheesh, –</em> he drawn out tiresomely,  <em>– Well I'm sorry for infringing on your woman! But surely you can agree that she</em> is<em> quite a steal. If anything, you should feel flattered there's so much competition.</em></p>

<p><em>– I... uh... Sorry, what exactly do you mean by that?</em></p>

<p>He came to a complete stop and took a good look at a genuinely thrown off Willow before his voice bloomed with a new, eager brand of amusement. </p>

<p>– <em>Well I've heard women tend to be short-sighted, but this is just pure comedy.</em></p>

<p><em>– Stop it, – </em>she barked back, defensive and frustrated, as she put another step of distance between the two of them, – <em>First that demon berates me, and now you, too! I'm trying my best to save her, okay? I am <u>trying</u>. And I want to hear <u>nothing</u> about it from you!</em></p>

<p><em>– Ah, well, that explains it, – </em>he lets out a short, obnoxious laugh as he pulls on the foyer's door handle, – <em>I'm</em> dying<em> to see how you'll </em>rescue <em>her. Bloody good show...</em></p>

<p>There was a palpable moment of silence between the scraping of the mechanism and the door being pushed open. But somehow, it didn't take him even the first step inside before the resident ghosts booed him with roaring upset.</p>

<p>– <em>Here comes the philistine! – </em>a man in the middle cried out, distained, – <em>Couldn't get enough troubling the diva, so he must now disturb her friend. </em></p>

<p><em>– Oh shut up, – </em>he groaned back at him, pushing himself through their wall of shoulders, – <em>Your policing only ever helped to spoil everyone's mood around here! </em></p>

<p><em>– And save miss Belle from certain </em>someone's<em> unwanted attention </em>thrice<em>, –</em> another ghostly voice cut in with rather scolding use of inflictions, <em>– Mind you, this week </em>alone<em>! </em></p>

<p><em>– Sure, stroke your ego plenty, – </em>he hissed, slamming the door behind him with a force of a particularly upset draft. The three ghosts quickly murmured more head-shaking disapprovals before their discussion swayed back towards their favorite topic : the singer. Willow let out a short exhale with a shadow of comfort as she joined the three by the wall.</p>

<p>– <em>Are you Belle's close friend?</em> – one of the ghosts opens a conversation.</p>

<p>For once, she was almost grateful that the undead kin was rather predictable.</p>

<p>– <em>Yes, looking out for her in light of recent brewing scandals, – </em>she stated matter-of-factly. </p>

<p>– <em>Oh my! – </em>another one joined in, – <em>Does that concern today's bold choice of song? I was thrilled with the news — we haven't heard Diamond Note in ages!</em></p>

<p><em>– I wouldn't go as far as to call it a "choice", no.</em></p>

<p>The trio froze under the coldness of her response. Willow continued, so grim it sounded almost gravely.</p>

<p>– <em>The lyrics were taken out of her chamber by Seven Clubs at the break of dawn, with no consent given. I also recall he's the one currently sitting at the piano, correct?</em></p>

<p><em>– ...well... maybe the staff is encouraging her to express her feelings? – </em>a shy voice suggested,<em> – Ever since she first written it, it never left her chamber's walls...  Such a shame for such divine music!..</em></p>

<p>Her short exhale sounded almost offended at their implication, momentarily rendering the ghostly recipient somewhat ashamed. The voodoo queen's cold gaze reminded completely unfaltered as she ungloved her right hand. Three sets of eyes transfixed on her dark bruises, deep and distinct enough one could count every finger. </p>

<p><em>– I have all reasons to believe she is being set up for something more sinister, – </em>she pressed with unchallenged certainty. </p>

<p><em>– How brutal! – </em>one ghost gasped. <br />
– <em>Manhandling a lady? – </em>the other fumed. <br />
– <em>But what compelled him? – </em>asked third with terror.</p>

<p>– <em>He did this because I demanded he surrenders the stolen lyrics, –</em> Willow painted the picture, – <em>What do you believe he will do when she refuses to sing them in front of the stage?</em></p>

<p>– <em>Oh, this is outrageous! – </em>a familiar voice of the pervert joined in the chorus, – <em>That bastard locked me out of the concert hall!</em></p>

<p><em>– Wait, what? – </em>Willow's head snapped to him with sheer surprise. She could swear that didn't happen last time. Did Seven Clubs anticipate she'd try to stop him?</p>

<p>– <em>Serves you right for ogling the singer, – </em>another ghost replied with no sympathy. <br />
– <em>Was he expecting you? –  </em>third hesitated.</p>

<p><em>– I'm expecting to snatch his damn key, I'll tell you that, – </em>the pervert grumbled, heading straight for the Dressing room's door, – <em>For 'effin sake, to lock </em>me <em>out of somewhere...</em></p>

<p><em>– Criminal, – </em>one from the trio couldn't hold back his critique.</p>

<p>– I <em>prefer "m</em><em>ore proactive than </em>you"<em>, – </em>the philistine barked, tried the handle and, to his own glee, found out it was left unlocked, <em>– A-ha, service entrance!</em></p>

<p>As the door slammed, three ghosts rippled with an offended wave of whispers, their attention steadily shifting from the display of improper behavior back to the core problem that led to it.</p>

<p><em>– Willow, miss? – </em>finally feeling ready, one of them invited her attention.<br />
<em>– We see what you're trying to say, – </em>the other begun, <em>– And we agree that we should aid you in the matter.</em><br />
<em>– So we think we'll wait and see when will be the right time to intervene, – </em>concluded the last</p>

<p>For once, their answer made her feel hopeful.</p>

<p>– <em>So you'll apprehend him? – </em>she confirmed, a profound relief touching her features, – <em>Thank you so, so much—</em></p>

<p>The hope came crashing down fast when the ghostly trio gasped in return.</p>

<p><em>– Violence? We intend no such thing.<br />
– We could stand between them, maybe...?<br />
– It would be ungentlemanly!</em></p>

<p><em>– What would be ungentlemanly about restraining the attacker? – </em>Willow raised an eyebrow, – <em>You're not dealing with a gentleman. Seven Clubs is a</em> predator.<em> He wants to </em>exploit<em> her, and he's not above hurting others to do so. You wouldn't want Tequila to walk into whatever trap he set up, would you?</em></p>

<p><em>– Well, what if it </em>was <em>a misunderstanding? – </em>a voice hesitated back.<br />
– <em>I think we might be jumping the gun with conclusions—<br />
</em></p>

<p><em>– Stop—</em></p>

<p>Willow found her voice climbing up involuntarily as her patience thinned dangerously close to a snapping point. She felt like she's been fighting everyone and everything from the moment she woke up, and her heart couldn't bear seeing her effort tumble down like a castle of cards at the finish line. She raised up a hand and forced herself to take a breath in and out before she voiced her frustration. </p>

<p><em>– Correct me if I'm wrong, but what you're saying is that you'd rather wait and see her get hurt than act on the evidence? Does this –</em> she pointed at the bruise, <em>– mean nothing to you?</em></p>

<p><em>– Not at all! It's rather that— We can't just play vigilantes! – </em>one stumbled for answers.<em><br />
– Yes, you can't just respond with violence to violence! – </em>supported the second.<br />
<em>– ...I think I've heard someone! – </em>briskly mentioned the third.</p>

<p><em>– Seriously, – </em>Williw lost her temper <em>– You have nothing left to lose than your pride, and yet— </em><em>Am </em><u>I</u><em> willing to risk more for her sake than <u>any</u> of you?</em></p>

<p>It was right in the middle of those words as she heard the door creep open behind her. As the voodoo queen regretfully recalled, it was, indeed, right about time the singer took her stage.<br />
It took just a moment for the two women's eyes to lock; it proved enough for Willow's eyes to cycle from surprise to fear, and for Tequila's worried expression to deepen in it's furrow further. </p>

<p>– <em>Oh, please,</em> – the singer exhaled, <em>– You just </em>can't <em>let it rest, can you?</em></p>

<p>– <em>I see nothing straining in listening to your rehearsal.</em></p>

<p><em>– Uh-huh. And all that monologue about restrainin' a man wasn't meant to be taken seriously?</em></p>

<p><em>– You didn't forbid me to secure help from the ghosts.</em></p>

<p><em>– Ghosts—</em></p>

<p>Remorsefully, Tequila swallowed her words; in a split second she decided she wasn't going to debate Willow's own beliefs at <em>this </em>point. With a sight, she complained,  – <em>God knows, you really </em>are <em>anxious.</em></p>

<p><em>– I can't permit him to lay a finger on you.</em></p>

<p>Tequila tipped her head, captivated by that thought. A curious, tired smile touched a corner of her mouth.</p>

<p>– <em>Should I be takin' your promises to save me from some unknown man quite as literally as they sound, darlin'?</em></p>

<p>– <em>I cannot put it any more literally.</em></p>

<p>The smile remained, but Tequila appeared momentarily at a loss for words.</p>

<p>– <em>I see.</em></p>

<p>– <em>You sound as though you don't, – </em>Willow murmured, defensive. </p>

<p>– <em>Oh, I may be beginning to.</em></p>

<p>A record scratched in an adjacent room, putting the music to a stop. The singer took it as her queue to wrap up with the congregating. </p>

<p>– <em>Must be Redd. I'll be on my way. Try not to eavesdrop, alright, darlin'?</em></p>

<p>The dark-eyed woman assured her with a nod before she properly processed what, and why, was asked of her. As the door closed behind Tequila, the implication finally sank on her chest uncomfortably.</p>

<p>–...<em>We know you feel hurt, miss, but we insist on our decision, – </em>trio's unnamed leader confessed a few seconds later.</p>

<p>– ..<em>.no objections to that, – </em>Willow lowered her eyes.</p>

<p>– <em>Oh no, all objections, actually! – </em>the perverted ghost proclaimed, slipping back into the foyer with a shiny red key dangling in the pocket of his undercoat, – <em>Are you all more concerned with debating ethics than </em>actually<em> doing anything?</em></p>

<p>Left at a loss of words, all of their eyes snapped to him as he took a confident stride towards the main entrance and leaned down to the keyhole.</p>

<p>– <em>She asked not to— </em></p>

<p><em>– Bah! – </em>the perverted ghost objected, as if anticipating the useless sentiment, – <em>It's for her </em>safety<em> and whatnot. How are you going to know the right timing to break in and catch the man red-handed?</em></p>

<p><em>– She'll certainly scream if the help is needed, – </em>one of the ghosts reasoned. </p>

<p>– ...<em>I hate to agree with him, –</em> Willow interjected grimly, <em>– but the point at which we'd hear her scream might be too late for saving. </em>Someone<em> has to watch. It might as well be</em> him<em>.</em></p>

<p><em>– You all really are no fun... – </em>he smirked to the ripple of helpless complaints and adjusted his position more comfortably. Soon, the perver eagerly broke the silence with his first announcement:</p>

<p>– <em>Tequila's making her entrance. Oh, she spotted him. Oh, she looks pissed!</em></p>

<p><em>"...where is Redd?...",</em><em> </em>her muffled voice carried through the door. The participants exchanged short glances. They strained to hear the conversation, or,  perhaps, politely pretended not to follow it, but certain heated words reached them even then. Philistine was glad to fill in the blanks when volume fell down to civil.</p>

<p>– <em>He's trying to laugh it off, –</em> the ghost narrated, <em>– She's </em>not<em> buying it.</em></p>

<p><em>"...not agree to this. I am <u>leaving,</u>" </em>a new statement reached them somewhat louder than before. </p>

<p>"...<em>But miss Belle!"</em> Seven Club pleaded in response, "<em>The show has already started!..."</em></p>

<p><em>"..."</em></p>

<p><em>"...Excuse me?..."</em></p>

<p><em>"I said <u>no</u>. I am <u>not</u> performing this."</em></p>

<p><em>"But...!", </em>Seven's voice disappeared behind the scraping of the chair and ghost's excited gasping. Narrator appeared a smidge more excited as he announced that the pianist abandoned the piano. A few urgent steps behind the door gave them a rough idea what for.</p>

<p>"...<em>I wish them all the happiness," </em>Tequila's voice calmly cut through. </p>

<p>For a second, nobody moved.</p>

<p>Then, the footsteps came down like thunder.</p>

<p>"<em>...a two-faced siren— I'm <u>not</u> having this from you!</em>"</p>

<p>– <em>Finally! –</em> philistine proclaimed with triumph, <em>– That bastard showed his face!</em></p>

<p><em>"Hands OFF of me," </em>Tequila threatened to scream.</p>

<p><em>"Get. On. The. Stage!"</em>, Seven Clubs pressed back on her.</p>

<p>– <em>She slapped him! –</em> the pervert was nearly drowning in excitement, <em>– </em><em>Ohoho, he got her hands right behind her back...!</em></p>

<p>– <em>Open the door! – </em>Willow hissed out a whisper.</p>

<p>– <em>But w</em><em>e just got to the good part—</em></p>

<p>– <em>Enough, – </em>a quieter gentleman proclaimed, heavily resting his ghostly arm on pervert's shoulder, – <em>The lady is </em>clearly <em>in distress. </em></p>

<p>A momentary flicker of fear on his face quickly changed into a sick sense of triumph as he pulled away and theatrically turned the key in the door. Under his breath, he taunted :</p>

<p>– <em>Then give him Hell, "gentlemen".</em></p><hr>

<p>Diamond Note. A neat little gimmick, special enough to elevate a singer to the status of national treasure and forever breed rumors regarding it's authenticity. Something heartbroken Tequila felt would've rubbed it in the face of that heartless, cold man that traded her for a caricaturist. It was a bad idea to perform that song, she now realised, as her face was pressed against the cold glass of the vista Marquis paned the orchestra pit over with "for safety". What does Lucas even know about safety? Turning her beautiful stage into a suffocating death trap. She should've trusted Willow's foresight. The glass...</p>

<p>She had to hold back her scream when she sensed the glass cracking.</p>

<p>– <em>The show must conclude, – </em>Seven Clubs hissed at her ear with seething anger, like an artist that saw their idea lit on fire and humiliated. There was more rage than power in his hold as he pressed on her body pinned against the fragile wall.</p>

<p>One moment, it felt like this is where it ends for her. The second, she heard the door slamming open, with an oh-so-familiar voice proclaiming:</p>

<p>– <em>Get him! </em></p>

<p><em>– What‽ – </em>the pianist was momentarily taken aback, staring in sheer disbelief. </p>

<p>She didn't see what hit his shoulder, or twisted away his hand from hers. Didn't catch how his back hit the stained glass window, but screamed as she felt his grip wrapping around her dress. In a flash that rendered the panes into colourful shards spraying over the vast space of the amphitheatre, they undid all false safety that Marquis intended for this place. In another, she felt the man's falling body dragging her into the void together with him. </p>

<p>She did not expect the fragile, ungloved hand with black bruises to be the one that caught her. Nor did she expect it to actually hold her as her dress ripped under the formidable weight of her intended murderer, sending him crashing down with a heavy "twump". For a few agonizing seconds, all she could see was Willow's terrified eyes, hands straining to pull her back and lips moving in unknown prayer. What came next would've been the display of inhuman strength she couldn't imagine her fragile frame possessing. </p>

<p>When Tequila found herself in her crushing embrace on the ornate floor of her stage room, she was ready to believe that the "voodoo queen" held the title for a reason. It came at no surprise, then, that her first words of reassurance were not meant for her.</p><hr>

<p>– <em>We only meant to separate them, – </em>the gentleman's leader apologized, voice sunk and hollow. </p>

<p>– <em>Separate you did, –</em> the philistine mocked them, <em>– So well, in fact, he ain't coming back for her!</em></p>

<p><em>– ...you did your best, – </em>Willow heaved, hyperventilating still, – <em>It was an accident. </em></p>

<p><em>– Clearly, we didn't do well enough! – </em>another ghostly voice lost it's temper, gesturing at the broken stained glass. They erupted in another worried discussion, <br />
She could relate to that panic a bit too well.<br />
Just a second. If she was just a second too late, she'd see Tequila plummeting. Hell, she already almost saw her shatter the glass face-first. She thought she would...</p>

<p>– <em>Willow, –</em> her voice strained a little, <em>– You can loosen the grip.</em></p>

<p>She pulled away, dropping a quick apology, as she suddenly became acutely aware of the woman laying on her chest. Her nervous eyes scanned the singer up and down, taking note of the cracked, skewed mask, tears that ripped up her dark-blue dress and small bleeding scratches left by the loose fragments of glass. Tequila looked startled, just as much as she was.<br />
But alive.</p>

<p><em>– We shall get someone, – </em>at last, the leader excused, receiving a short nod from Willow.<br />
The three rushed right out of the door. Not the philistine, no, but it was probably for the better someone stayed behind.</p>

<p><em>– ...a..are you alright? – </em>her voice came out desperate as she reached to pull off her mask, <em>– Did you get hurt? Please—<br />
– I could ask you the same.<br />
<br />
</em>She found her hand stopped by Tequila's gentle, yet firm, grip, and soon felt her satin glove brushing streaming tears off her face. Only then did she realize what state she's been in : shaking, crying, and utterly terrified. It took a moment before the singer's smile offered her enough reassurance to stop suffocating. </p>

<p><em>– There, there... – </em>she whispered, <em>– It's alright, darlin'. I'm alright.</em></p>

<p><em>– ...you are, – </em>she repeated in disbelief.</p>

<p>Willow found herself growing much quieter. Tenderly, Tequila took off her skull-shaped mask, wiping off the last tears off the weary eyes. She felt a shiver run down her back as the draft from the gaping maw of the new, opened void pulled at the room; deadly lyrics, abandoned at the pianist's seat, fell gently onto the floor at the wind's tug.</p>

<p>- <em>So much trouble over one miserable song, - </em>Tequila laughed joylessly, seeing her Shattered Hopes scattering on the floor among the rare, colourful shards. <br />
- <em>Was it ever about the song?</em><em> - </em>her question came back hollow, but with no judgement in it.</p>

<p>Something in singer's expression shifted in a way Willow couldn't quite place. Her exhaustion gave way to something warmer. Gratitude, or, perhaps, recognition.</p>

<p><em>- You really thought you would lose me, didn't you? - </em>she asked with a hint of a smile, looking her straight in the eye.</p>

<p>- <em>I almost </em>did <em>lose you</em>, - Willow corrected bitterly, -<em> There were hundred of ways this could've went so much worse. I was barely on time to help you. God knows, I shouldn't have left your side to begin with...!</em></p>

<p>She fell silent the moment Tequila's fingers brushed her cheek, wide-eyed stare of black eyes growing much more attentive. She didn't catch the reason why a satisfied smile bloomed on her face, as she whispered back:</p>

<p>- <em>What an impossible woman you are. You could've just told me.</em></p>

<p><em>- But... -</em> Willow felt her breath hitch, <em>- <em>I tried. I knew you'd never believe me...</em></em></p>

<p><em>- That's not what she asked, - </em>the philistine snickered, covering up his face in anticipation.</p>

<p>Just that momentary distraction rendered her completely unprepared for when Tequila suddenly leaned in <em>much</em> closer, filling the air around her with the sweet scent of her body mist and completely capturing her view. A surprised gasp that was about to escape her was suddenly captured by something soft resting over her lips. <br />
She found herself caught completely off-guard by the new development she didn't envision happening. A moment that felt like it lasted forever, but ended before she understood what to do <em>— hell</em>, how she felt about it, even. All she was left with was a soft pressure against her mouth, brief enough that Willow might later have questioned whether it happened at all were it not for how completely stunned she was left by the time Tequila pulled away.<br />
Willow stared into her eyes helplessly.<br />
Tequila stared back.</p>

<p>One second.</p>

<p>Two.</p>

<p>Three.</p>

<p>The philistine burst into delighted applause.<br />
– <em>Oh, magnificent! Absolutely magnificent! This was</em> so <em>worth all the goddamn trouble I went through for you!</em></p>

<p>Willow felt her face swelling with an unfamiliar shade of red. – <em>Not a word, – </em>she whispered, staring the philistine down.<br />
Tequila caught her looking away, and a smile that suddenly crossed her face bloomed into a quiet, relieved laughter that carried away the rest of the tension near-death experience left her with. <br />
<em>– There's no shame in it bein' your first time.<br />
<br />
</em>The apt conclusion met her like a cold shower. At a loss of words, she covered up her face with her palms.</p>

<p><em>– Can't believe it! –</em> the pervert huffed, <em>– You really </em>are <em>a prude!</em></p>

<p><em>– Shut up, – </em>she whispered back, haven't yet decided if the message concerned them both. She found herself suddenly exhausted from everything that happened so far, and didn't feel like she had it left in her to do any more arguing.</p>

<p>The peaceful silence that followed, punctuated by the ticks of the clock in the foyer, was gently interrupted by someone's unhurried, but purposeful footsteps. They both looked towards the door, alert of the entrance of the new staff member <em>— King Clubs, </em>she took note, <em>—</em> that now studied the state of the room. Despite of her initial reaction, Willow convinced herself to take it calmly: It only made sense someone would check up on the accident scene. <em>Especially</em> with all the noise they've made.  </p>

<p><em>– Miss Belle. Miss Blue, – </em>he recognized them, keeping his voice courteous, <em>– Did something happen?</em></p>

<p><em>– Thank goodness you're here! – </em>Tequila breathed with relief, straightening out her back,  <em>– There has been an accident. We need to notify Lucas, and fetch the doctor.</em></p>

<p><em>– Of course, Miss Belle, – </em>he responded with a short bow, before turning back to the door, <em>– Please, remain here. </em></p>

<p>Seeing how the world just kept moving forward despite and because of all that happened, Willow suddenly felt her caught breath escape with a bit of relief. She did it. She solved the riddle and broke free of the cursed loop. There'll surely be time to get through the rest. There will always be time.<br />
Without realizing it, she held onto her twin doll again. Progress came with reassurance.<br />
Seeing her confident, Tequila allowed herself a tired smile, too.</p>

<p>It caught them by surprise when King Clubs returned alone with nothing but an elongated instrument in his arms and hastily twisted the key in the door.</p>

<p><em>– I'm terribly sorry for the disrupted show.<br />
– A soprano sax won't be of much use here, – </em>Tequila objected, before King Club's determined stride in her direction startled her to raise up her voice, <em>– Excuse me! What are you—</em></p>

<img src="https://64.media.tumblr.com/aa589df8875a7082c2efa477804da7f6/527e9990a7192707-63/s1280x1920/a2c644c3c00e51d47e20a9f883d3b29996495ac8.jpg" width="666" height="auto">


<p>When the swung brass shape of the instrument disappeared out of her sight, Willow forgot to react. The impact that connected at the back of her head was so sudden and devastating she barely had time to register sudden taste of her blood, or the pain. As her vision clouded and legs lost their hold, certain familiar voice suddenly breathed with relief :</p>

<p align="center"><em><strong>[[There, child. The script fell back into it's place.|Chapter 4: Terms and Conditions]]</strong></em></p>


:: Chapter 4: Terms and Conditions {"position":"300,1000","size":"100,100"}
<p>She finds herself jolted awake by sudden splitting headache, more upset than surprised by the outcome. Willow sat up in her bed and tensed the uncooperative fingers in drawn-out motions, reminding her body that death doesn't really stick with it. Soon after, rested her hand on the matchbox in a familiar gesture. Considered lighting the candle, but soon decided she'd rather not hold another conversation with Grinmaw at this point.</p>

<p>After all, what would it even say? Something cruel and mocking she was in no mood to listen to, no doubt.</p>

<p>Instead, she laid back onto the familiar mattress and stared blankly at the ceiling. After a momentary hesitation, touched her lips, and then frowned at herself over how much she wished she could remember what exactly it was like. At this point, the moment blurred into a shameful fright of everything that followed it. She couldn't say if she liked it. She couldn't say if she liked <em>her</em>. She never even been kissed before, Tequila's apt remark be damned. The whole situation was so new and unexpected to her that Willow outright failed to find a framework to process it - and that alone annoyed her as much as it flustered her.</p>

<p>How long has she felt for her that way? Should she have noticed sooner? No, god, she's getting ahead of herself - did Tequila even have feelings for her? Or was it just the spur of the moment thing? She looked at her as if the kiss was the only natural response. Did she herself do something to make her think that way? Because that ghost, too-</p>

<p>Willow let out a long, frustrated sigh, hiding her face behind her hands. She didn't have time to solve any personal mysteries. She should be more worried about inevitable death that still awaited her and everyone she cared about. So why, then, did it suddenly matter so much?<br />
She shut her eyes and forced herself to take a deep breath, and then another. Finding her head stubbornly refusing to clear up, she hesitantly decided that it might not be <em>all that bad </em>to confront the thoughts instead of shutting them out entirely. And if all of this came just under the darn Guppy's influence (a possibility she paranoidally considered after a certain showstopper), well, it won't be getting to witness her embarrassing self-tormenting either way.</p>

<p>Willow wasn't too familiar with attraction, be it hers or anyone else's. So, how <em>did</em> it feel, really?</p>

<p>Well, for once, rather awkward. </p>

<p>Tequila wasn't a complete stranger to her. A friend, yes. Not exactly what people call a close one, but definitely closer than anyone else around here. For as much as she hates to admit it, she's been following her like a dog on a leash. But that's just because she couldn't bear to see her get hurt, and, worse, do nothing about it, It's not love, or, at least, no more than the kind of love one feels for their close family.<br />
She just couldn't handle any more people dying on her hands. Not after...</p>

<p>Willow felt herself frowning at the thought, shifting side restlessly. Maybe she should try a different angle.</p>

<p>Would it even be weird to have feeling for her? Objectively, Tequila was exceptionally beautiful. Maybe not exactly in Willow's taste, but she won't stand above admitting she could see why many found her desirable. She had certain social grace and allure Willow lacked, that ability to become the center of the stage and capture audience - a whole three followers devoting their afterlife to her were enough to testify that. And she felt much more comfortable with this kind of attention, too. After all, there was a good reason why Tequila found it acceptable and appropriate to kiss her in response to a misunderstood confession. Willow could have never. But that's precisely what bothered her - a woman that seemingly stood above all else somehow chose somebody as awkward as her.</p>

<p>No, really. What gives?</p>

<p>Tequila genuinely seems to care about her. In a purely friendly way, yes, but it's more about the kind of kindness and attention she offers to a, frankly, social outcast Willow tends to be in Brutale's loud company. She seems to care. Not understand, no, there's barely anyone who ever could. But she cares enough to try and see the picture. Or, at least, respect boundaries, where they are, and intervene where she should. <br />
Not indifferent, in just the way Willow could appreciate.</p>

<p>She bit her lip, realizing that she didn't manage to arrive at a concrete answer at all. She hoped to find a "no, absolutely not", personally, but she couldn't deny her own wavering still remained. </p>

<p>Guess she'll just have to wait and see if there was more to the misunderstanding. If it even remained after the time erased itself yet again.</p>

<p>Amidst her thinking, she suddenly caught the sound of Tequila's voice quietly bleed through the wall. If she tried hard enough, she could piece out the haunting, beautiful lyrics of Shattered Hopes.</p>

<p>Right. This must be when she writes down the song – after when she usually leaves to argue with the Guppy. She felt stupid now, missing that detail time and time again.</p>

<p>She remembered the midnight conversation from the previous time and wondered if it was her turn to offer back some comfort. Maybe talking would do well for both of them? She didn't know Tequila and her personal drama all too well, so she might as well take her chances to get more familiar. </p>

<p> </p>

<p>She announced her presence at Tequila's door with a short knock. In a second, she was seeing Tequila face to face. Disturbed. Somehow, not questioning that it's Willow she'd find standing there even for a second. </p>

<p>– <em>Sorry for the noise,</em> – she apologized preemptively, – <em>I'll keep it down.</em></p>

<p>– <em>Don't apologize, I couldn't get much sleep, either,</em> - she deflected, falling thoughtfully silent for a beat, -<em> It sounded beautiful, by the way. The song.</em></p>

<p>- <em>It's nothing good, really... Just needed to throw it out of my head and onto the paper,</em> - she rambled on, as if trying to put a distance between herself and the script, - <em>I couldn't fall asleep otherwise.</em></p>

<p><em>- What were you writing? If you'll allow me to pry. </em></p>

<p>Tequila recoiled for a second, a shameful doubt crossing her features. Her first instinct seemed to retreat, and the one that followed immediately after - berate herself for treating the song as if she wrote something illegal. In the end, the desire to be seen won over the desire to appear decent. Perhaps, Grinmaw's play. Perhaps, just who Tequila was as a person.</p>

<p>–<em> ...Would you like to come in?</em> - she finally offered her answer, leaning away from the doorframe.</p>

<p>–<em> M-hm. Would you like some coffee?</em></p>

<p><em>- At one AM? - </em>she raised her eyebrows with no real surprise behind the gesture.</p>

<p><em>- Well, then... no need.</em></p>

<p>Tequila's room screamed modesty in a way her dresses and hairdo would make an observer deny outright. The truth is, though, that she didn't need a constant reminder of being a diva in everyone's eyes. Perhaps, she didn't even like the pedestal to begin with. Willow was only left guessing what was it like to see the world through the eyes of the centerpiece of Lucas's stage, as opposed to her modest role hidden amongst the amazed crowd. </p>

<p>----------------------------------------</p>

<p>Plan for the chapter :<br />
- Willow wakes up and contemplates what had just happened.<br />
- Willow hears Tequila on the other side, singing, and realizes that's when she writes down "Shattered Hopes"<br />
- Willow gets up and knocks on Tequila's door. A short conversation follows. Willow asks if Tequila would be willing to share some about the song, and Tequila, feeling shameful for it, but even more so wanting to be seen, agrees for a chat.<br />
- Willow ends up in Tequila's room. They open an empty chat about the water leaking through the wall, which evolves into shared complains about Lucas sucking at keeping things in check, which finally leads to the question of how do they feel about Lucas, personally. Tequila shares a bit of a backstory and old grudges.<br />
- Finally, they reach the topic of the song itself. Tequila admits she felt very bitter, condescendingly laughs that Lucas, an insensible stunt man that he is, would probably love to hear her pouring dirt on him, but then immediately regrets it once she thinks of Eleanor and her innocence in this muddy situation between them and how she'd hate to drag her down to such level.<br />
- Willow doesn't really offer any input on the matter so far, only asking questions to let Tequila figure out how she feels. She probes, "Why do you want him to hear it?", "You want to be seen, yes, but why like this?", "What made him so important for you?"<br />
- Tequila slowly arrives at a realization that she doesn't really want to look at herself as someone who was besides Lucas anymore, and that the ship has, in fact, sailed long ago for there to be any reasons to keep on holding.<br />
- Willow shyly says that there are far better people out there that would care about her much more, and Lucas doesn't deserve a fraction of attention she'd give him heartbreaking over his indifference.<br />
- An easy silence falls between two for a moment, until one of them suddenly offers "Maybe let's just burn it?". Willow gladly offers Tequila her matchbox. They watch the paper turn to ashes.<br />
- After wishing each other goodnight, both fall asleep easily. Willow pleasantly imagines Grinmaw's or Seven Club's upset faces.<br />
- Willow wakes up an hour or two earlier than usual, and out of caution decides she should probably check if Seven Club didn't pull anything else weird during the night. On her way to the Music Hall, she suddenly spots Sixpence rushing to the chapel, looking around cautiously as if somebody's after him. Perplexed, she decides to follow in his footsteps.<br />
- Willow stalks him at a distance where Sixpence wouldn't notice her, trying to peek through the keyholes to get a grasp of what's going on. Somehow, she gets a feeling she's witnessing something rather important, and inserting herself into it wouldn't work well for Sixpence.<br />
- As she observes Sixpence enter the chapel, she very suddenly feels the side of Lucas's old hunting rifle put against her neck with a violent force, before it ultimately choked her/was used as a leverage to snap her neck. She saw the gloved hands of the staff. She couldn't pick out who was behind the act. The death came too suddenly, and not even Grinmaw commented this time.</p>


